// Plotting demonstration for the \use{plot} package.
//
// Render with:  texish scripts/plot.script -o plot -p letter
//
// The plot package is built entirely in the document language over the \picture
// vector-graphics layer: \plot sets up a data-to-device transform, draws the axes,
// tick marks, numeric labels, grid and title, then runs its body to draw the data
// series on top. Series come in four kinds — \lineplot, \scatter, \bars and \fnplot —
// each taking an optional [colour] and [label] before its data, plus reference lines,
// a legend, an auto colour cycle, and automatic ranging.

\use{plot}

\def headline {\italic{Plot demo} \hfil page \the\pageno}
\def footline {\hfil — \the\pageno — \hfil}

\font lmroman 12 regular

\bold{\TeXish{} plots — data graphics in the document}

A \italic{plot} is a \picture with a coordinate system: you give the data ranges and
a few series, and the package maps data values to the page, draws the axes and labelled
ticks, and strokes the series. Because it is all the document language, the prose, the
axes and the curve share one rendering pipeline.

\bold{A line series with a legend}

Series that are given a \italic{[label]} record a legend entry; \italic{\\legend} then
draws the key. A \italic{\\scatter} drops a marker at each point, a \italic{\\lineplot}
strokes a polyline, and both read the same flat \italic{x y x y …} list.

\xrange{0}{6}
\yrange{0}{130}
\xlabel{time (s)}
\ylabel{height (m)}
\plottitle{Projectile}
\plot{
  \lineplot[royalblue][trajectory]{0 0  1 50  2 88  3 112  4 124  5 126  6 118}
  \scatter[crimson][samples]{1 50  3 112  5 126}
  \legend[se]
}

\bold{A bar chart with a reference line}

\italic{\\bars} draws a vertical bar to each value; \italic{\\hline} (and
\italic{\\vline}) draw a dashed reference line at a data value — here the mean.

\xrange{0}{7}
\yrange{0}{160}
\xlabel{quarter}
\ylabel{revenue}
\plottitle{Quarterly revenue}
\plot{
  \bars[teal]{1 95  2 110  3 80  4 140  5 120  6 150}
  \hline[crimson]{115.8}
}

\eject

\bold{Auto colour and marker shapes}

With no \italic{[colour]}, series take colours from a palette in turn. The marker
shape is set with \italic{plotmarkshape} (circle, square, triangle, diamond).

\xrange{0}{10}
\yrange{0}{10}
\plottitle{Three scatter series}
\set plotmarkr {3.2}
\plot{
  \set plotmarkshape {circle}   \scatter[][alpha]{1 2  3 5  5 4  7 8  9 6}
  \set plotmarkshape {square}   \scatter[][beta]{1 5  3 3  5 7  7 5  9 9}
  \set plotmarkshape {triangle} \scatter[][gamma]{1 8  3 7  5 9  7 6  9 4}
  \legend[nw]
}

\bold{A function plot}

\italic{\\fnplot} samples a function of \italic{x} across the domain and strokes the
result. The expression is ordinary \italic{\\calc} arithmetic in terms of \italic{x}.

\xrange{-3}{3}
\yrange{0}{9}
\xlabel{$x$}
\ylabel{$y$}
\plottitle{$y = x^2$}
\plot{
  \fnplot[seagreen]{x*x}
}

\bold{Two curves, a zero axis, and forced steps}

When a range straddles zero the package draws a light axis line there. Several series
share one plot, and the tick step can be pinned with \italic{\\xstep} / \italic{\\ystep}.

\xrange{0}{6.283}
\yrange{-1.2}{1.2}
\xstep{1}
\ystep{0.5}
\xlabel{angle (rad)}
\plottitle{Sine and cosine}
\plot{
  \fnplot[][sin]{sin(x)}
  \fnplot[][cos]{cos(x)}
  \legend[se]
}

\bold{Automatic ranging}

\italic{\\autorange} scans the data and chooses the ranges, padding the y axis and
resting it on a zero baseline — no \italic{\\xrange} / \italic{\\yrange} needed.

\xlabel{day}
\ylabel{count}
\plottitle{Auto-ranged}
\autorange{1 12  2 19  3 15  4 27  5 22  6 31  7 28}
\plot{
  \lineplot[darkorange][visits]{1 12  2 19  3 15  4 27  5 22  6 31  7 28}
  \legend[se]
}

\eject

\bold{Area and step series}

\italic{\\areaplot} fills the band under a curve to the baseline; \italic{\\stepplot}
holds each value until the next x, for piecewise-constant or cumulative data.

\xrange{0}{8}
\yrange{0}{50}
\xlabel{day}
\plottitle{Cumulative}
\plot{
  \areaplot[lightsteelblue]{0 0  1 8  2 14  3 23  4 30  5 38  6 44  7 48  8 50}
  \stepplot[crimson][rate]{0 8  1 6  2 9  3 7  4 8  5 6  6 4  7 2}
  \legend[nw]
}

\bold{A categorical axis with value labels}

\italic{\\xcategories} names the x ticks instead of numbering them, and
\italic{plotvalues} prints each bar's value above it.

\yrange{0}{160}
\ylabel{revenue}
\plottitle{By quarter}
\xcategories{Q1 Q2 Q3 Q4}
\set plotvalues {1}
\plot{
  \bars[teal]{1 95  2 130  3 80  4 150}
}
\set plotvalues {0}

\eject

\bold{Error bars and a trend line}

\italic{\\errorbars} draws a capped whisker of $y \pm$ error at each point, and
\italic{\\trendline} fits the least-squares line through the data and draws it dashed.

\xrange{0}{10}
\yrange{0}{60}
\xlabel{dose}
\ylabel{response}
\plottitle{Measured response}
\plot{
  \scatter[steelblue][mean]{1 8  3 17  5 24  7 39  9 47}
  \errorbars[steelblue]{1 8 3  3 17 5  5 24 4  7 39 6  9 47 5}
  \trendline[firebrick][fit]{1 8  3 17  5 24  7 39  9 47}
  \legend[se]
}

\bold{Minor ticks and tick-label formatting}

\italic{plotxminor} / \italic{plotyminor} add minor ticks and grid lines between the
majors, and \italic{\\xtickformat} / \italic{\\ytickformat} wrap the tick numbers — here
a \italic{\\\$} prefix on y and a \italic{\\\%} suffix on x.

\xrange{0}{100}
\yrange{0}{50}
\xlabel{utilisation}
\ylabel{cost}
\plottitle{Formatted axes}
\set plotxminor {2}
\set plotyminor {5}
\xtickformat{}{\%}
\ytickformat{\$}{}
\plot{
  \lineplot[seagreen]{0 5  20 9  40 16  60 25  80 38  100 47}
}
\set plotxminor {0}
\set plotyminor {0}

\eject

\bold{A bubble chart}

\italic{\\bubble} reads \italic{x y size} triples and draws a disc at each point whose
\italic{area} encodes the third value. The discs are translucent, so overlapping bubbles
show through one another and two series in one plot stay legible.

\xrange{0}{10}
\yrange{0}{10}
\xlabel{price}
\ylabel{rating}
\plottitle{Products by market share}
\plot{
  \bubble[royalblue][premium]{2 8 12  4 7 30  6 9 8  8 6 22  3 5 16}
  \bubble[darkorange][budget]{3 4 18  5 3 9  7 5 25  9 2 14  6 6 28}
  \legend[sw]
}

Each figure above is a single box that flows in the text like any other, so a plot can
sit inline with prose, in a figure float, or beside a table.
